pancake has no stable release yet. This table says what runs on every push,
whether that check crosses into code maintained outside the project, and what
it still leaves open. It is evidence, not a compatibility or security promise.

## Evidence by surface

| Surface | Evidence on every push | What remains |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Protocol core | The upstream OCapN suite and a live Goblins peer exercise V1.0 model, session, engine, calls, promises, and all three handoff roles. In-tree scenarios exercise the next-profile promise-listen behavior (OCapN PR 282) in `pancake-next`. | No outside peer exercises the next profile; community review lists the other exact gaps. |
| Syrup | Fixtures are regenerated with upstream Syrup encoders and compared byte for byte. | None known in the supported message set. |
| Cap'n Proto | In-tree golden frames and cross-codec tests run under `zig build check`. | No outside Cap'n Proto implementation reads pancake's hand-written format. |
| `tcp` | Live Goblins checks use cleartext TCP through a testing netlayer this repository wrote for the vat. Goblins ships onion, not TCP. The upstream OCapN suite uses its own `tcp-testing-only` symbol. | The channel is cleartext and the peer proves no identity. [Known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md) says why. |
| `iroh` | `iroh-interop` and the relay checks use real iroh endpoints and its QUIC stack. | Two-NAT hole-punching is not in CI. Goblins ships no iroh netlayer; `goblins-iroh` meets a vat through the adapter this repository wrote (needs cargo + racket; not in the every-push CI script `ci/gates.sh`). |
| `keyed-tcp` and `inproc` | In-tree restart, refusal, and end-to-end netlayer tests run under `check`. | No outside peer speaks these transport symbols; `inproc` is test-only. |
| `noise-tcp` | In-tree sealed-channel and netlayer tests run under `check`. The Zig host tiers, C driver loop, and all four native bindings dial and listen on it. | No outside CapTP peer speaks Noise; this is pancake-to-pancake. [Known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md) covers the blocking-tier refusal. |
| `keyed-relay` | In-tree netlayer tests run under `check`. On-demand rendezvous proofs (`bindings-keyed-relay-rendezvous`, `zig-keyed-relay-register`) cold-dial a registered non-listening peer through the reference relay. | Those rendezvous steps need deno and are not in the every-push list. The channel is cleartext and unauthenticated; [known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md) says why. |
| `noise-relay` | In-tree tests cover a live native provider dial and registration carrying an authenticated fetch through a plaintext-observed relay. The native binding rendezvous matrix exercises locator-carried routes, and a real Chromium wasm peer reads authenticated transport provenance from its session. | No outside CapTP peer speaks this pancake transport profile. The browser and cross-runtime checks need Deno, Racket, Cargo, or Chromium and are not all in the every-push list. |
| `onion` | Fake-daemon tests drive the blocking resident, provider lifecycle, native completion loop, and every native binding. Live checks cover two pancake peers, both Goblins directions, the complete portable upstream suite plus the strict abort probe, fresh-process service restoration, and Rooms with two independent clients. | Live Tor checks need `tor` and outbound network, so they sit beside the every-push list. No live scale run is recorded. [Known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md) holds the rest. |
| `webrtc` | Rust sidecar tests cover inbound staging backpressure, outbound prefix progress, oversized-message refusal, retained capacity, runtime teardown, readiness coalescing, and exactly-once terminal publication. | The readiness-driven native DataChannel, browser, and signaling-mesh checks are manual rather than CI runs. Host adoption and three-party redeem are proved by `webrtc-loop` and `webrtc-bridge-redeem` (need cargo; not in the every-push check list). |
| C ABI | Header parity, layouts, ownership, archives, and all binding consumers run in-tree. | There is no independent C consumer; live binding proofs still pass through this ABI. |
| Rust, Deno, Python, and Racket bindings | Each binding holds a live Goblins session as a client and in both handoff roles. | Only Deno has a live check in which Goblins dials the binding. |
| TypeScript on Bun | `bun-dogfood` (a binding check that exercises the C ABI as a real consumer) covers Host identity/lifetime, locator and sturdyref ABI calls, and fail-closed native-loop admission. | Bun's synchronous FFI cannot host the native driver or blocking transport lifecycles. No Bun-native Goblins check runs. |
| Native Zig driver and actor layer | Front-door session, host, handoff, turn-loop, hosting, and snapshot tests run under `check`. | These are pancake-native APIs with no outside implementation to compare. |
| Distributed GC | A live Goblins peer sends `op:gc-exports`; in-tree tests cover outbound release and `op:gc-answers`. | No live peer observes pancake's outbound GC or binding finalizers. |
| Relay | Every runtime runs TCP, backpressure, idle-reclaim, and iroh checks. The Deno relay also carries a live Goblins session. | The outside-peer proof does not run through the Python, Rust, or Racket relays. |
| CLI and MCP | In-tree command, exit-code, deadline, and listen/call tests run under `check`. | No independent tool drives the CLI as a compatibility test. |
| Browser WebAssembly | JavaScript-to-Wasm handshake and ABI checks run in CI. `ocapnviz-live-browser` drives the production bundle as a real Chromium OCapN peer on demand. | WebRTC and general cold-dial scenarios remain manual checks. |
| Linux | The full CI sequence runs on Linux. | No claim is made for untested kernel or architecture combinations. |
| macOS | Cross-compilation checks the library and kqueue backend. | Nothing executes on macOS in CI. |
| Windows | Nothing. | Windows and an IOCP completion backend are not implemented. |
| Hostile-input and mutation tests | Bounded malformed-input tests and coverage-guided mutation runs cover codecs, framing, sessions, handoff, locators, the host, actors, and persistent collections. | The per-push fuzz budget is shallow, and most assertions are written by pancake. |

Outside evidence is useful only for the boundary it crosses. A live iroh
endpoint checks the transport stack, not agreement with another CapTP
implementation. A live Goblins session checks protocol interop, not API
stability.

The detailed outside-peer matrix is in
[community review](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/community-review.md).
The evidence is current only when the
[checks workflow](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/actions/workflows/check.yml)
is green for the commit you are evaluating. The
[repository page](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/stability.md)
owns the every-push check list that this table summarizes against.

For feature coverage, read
[what ships](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/shipped.md).
For consequences that may change a deployment, read
[known limitations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/boundaries.md).
